C++ Base Reference With a Virtual Function
Description

This C++ program demonstrates run-time polymorphism using a base-class reference and a virtual area() function.

The Shape class contains a virtual area() function. The Rectangle and Circle classes inherit from Shape and provide their own implementations of area().

Classes Used
1. Shape Class

The Shape class is the base class.

It contains the virtual area() function.
The default implementation returns 0.0.
It contains a virtual destructor.
The virtual keyword enables run-time polymorphism.
2. Rectangle Class

The Rectangle class is derived from the Shape class.

length stores the length.
width stores the width.
The constructor initializes the length and width.
area() is overridden to calculate the rectangle's area.

Formula:

Area = Length × Width
3. Circle Class

The Circle class is also derived from the Shape class.

radius stores the radius.
The constructor initializes the radius.
area() is overridden to calculate the circle's area.

Formula:

Area = π × Radius × Radius
printArea() Function

The function:

void printArea(const Shape& shape)

accepts a reference to the base class.

Because area() is virtual, the appropriate derived-class function is called depending on the object passed to the function.

Main Function

In the main() function, two objects are created:

Rectangle → length 5.0, width 3.0
Circle → radius 2.0

They are passed to the printArea() function:

printArea(rectangle);
printArea(circle);

The correct area() function is selected at run time.

Output
Area: 15
Area: 12.5664
OOP Concept Used

Run-Time Polymorphism — Base-Class Reference

              Shape
                |
          virtual area()
             /      \
            /        \
     Rectangle       Circle
         |              |
      Area = 15      Area = 12.5664

The base-class reference can refer to different derived objects, and the appropriate overridden area() function is called during execution.